---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Renderer">
  <p>
    <code>@braccato/core</code> is a custom element, <code>&lt;braccato-lyrics&gt;</code>, that renders
    synced lyrics, highlights each syllable as it is sung, and scrolls to follow the song. It has no
    runtime dependencies. This page covers what most integrations need. The
    <a href="https://github.com/better-lyrics/braccato/tree/master/packages/core#readme" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">package README</a>
    has the full reference.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">{`npm i @braccato/core`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Imports</h2>
  <p>
    Importing <code>@braccato/core/element</code> registers the element. The package does not load its
    own CSS, so import the three stylesheets too. Load <code>variables.css</code> first, because the
    other two read from it.
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">{`import "@braccato/core/element";
import "@braccato/core/styles/variables.css";
import "@braccato/core/styles/lyrics.css";
import "@braccato/core/styles/instrumental.css";`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Without the stylesheets, the lines are in the document but unstyled. The element renders into
    light DOM, not a shadow root, so your page CSS reaches the lines.
  </p>

  <h2>Required CSS</h2>
  <p>The element has no <code>display</code> of its own. Set it:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-css">{`braccato-lyrics {
  display: block;
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The renderer measures the space the first line needs to reach the scroll position, but
    <code>lyrics.css</code> only applies the bottom half. Add the top:
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-css">{`.blyrics-container {
  padding-top: var(--blyrics-padding-top, 2rem);
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Scrolling</h2>
  <p>
    Autoscroll writes <code>scrollTop</code> on the nearest scrollable ancestor, starting at the
    element itself. An ancestor counts when its <code>overflow-y</code> is <code>auto</code> or
    <code>scroll</code>. If nothing scrolls, the renderer scrolls the whole page.
  </p>
  <p>To make the element its own scroller, give it a height:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-css">{`braccato-lyrics {
  display: block;
  height: 60vh;
  overflow-y: auto;
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    To scroll a different container, name it with <code>host</code>. Writing <code>host</code> rebuilds
    the view, so set it once during setup.
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">{`view.host = { getScrollElement: () => frame };`}</code></pre>

  <h3>Pausing autoscroll when the user scrolls</h3>
  <p>
    The element does not listen for scroll events. Tell the renderer when the user scrolls, or
    autoscroll pulls the view back on the next frame and <code>braccato:scroll-state</code> never fires:
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">{`view.addEventListener("scroll", () => view.renderer?.noteUserScroll(), { passive: true });`}</code></pre>
  <p>Listen on whichever element actually scrolls.</p>

  <h2>Driving playback</h2>

  <h3>From a media element</h3>
  <p>
    Set <code>source</code> to a CSS selector or to an <code>HTMLMediaElement</code>. A selector is
    resolved when the element connects, so the media element must already be in the document, or you
    set the property from script. While a source is bound, the element reads the time, paused state and
    playback rate on its own, and a line click seeks the media element. Writes to
    <code>currentTime</code> and <code>playing</code> are ignored in this mode.
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">{`view.source = document.querySelector("audio");`}</code></pre>

  <h3>From your own clock</h3>
  <p>
    Without a source, write <code>currentTime</code> (in <strong>seconds</strong>) and
    <code>playing</code> on every animation frame. If the song plays at a rate other than 1, pass it as
    <code>tickOptions.playbackRate</code>.
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">{`function tick() {
  view.currentTime = player.currentTimeSeconds;
  view.playing = !player.paused;
  requestAnimationFrame(tick);
}
requestAnimationFrame(tick);`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Properties</h2>
  <p>You can write every property before the element is in the document.</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Property</th>
        <th>Type</th>
        <th>Description</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>lyrics</code></td>
        <td><code>Lyric[] | null</code></td>
        <td>The lines to render, usually from <a href="/braccato/parsers"><code>@braccato/parsers</code></a>. An empty array clears the view</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>lyricsOptions</code></td>
        <td>object</td>
        <td>Build options: <code>loaderVisible</code>, <code>noLyrics</code>, <code>language</code> (a BCP 47 tag for the source lyrics)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>source</code></td>
        <td><code>string | HTMLMediaElement | null</code></td>
        <td>The media element to follow, or a selector for it</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>currentTime</code></td>
        <td><code>number</code></td>
        <td>Playback position in seconds</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>playing</code></td>
        <td><code>boolean</code></td>
        <td>Whether the song is playing. A paused view animates differently</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>tickOptions</code></td>
        <td>object</td>
        <td>Clock offsets, passive scrolling, sample time and <code>playbackRate</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>theme</code></td>
        <td><code>string</code></td>
        <td>A CSS stylesheet. See <a href="#theming">Theming</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>host</code></td>
        <td>object</td>
        <td>Overrides for what the renderer asks of the page, such as <code>getScrollElement</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>renderer</code></td>
        <td>read-only</td>
        <td>The renderer underneath, for methods like <code>noteUserScroll()</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>status</code></td>
        <td>read-only</td>
        <td><code>idle</code>, <code>rendering</code>, <code>theme-conflict</code> or <code>no-browsing-context</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The <code>source</code>, <code>theme</code>, <code>current-time</code> and <code>playing</code>
    attributes set the matching properties. Properties never write back to attributes.
  </p>

  <h2>Events</h2>
  <p>All events bubble and are composed.</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Event</th>
        <th>Detail</th>
        <th>When</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>braccato:lyrics-loaded</code></td>
        <td>{`{ lineCount, syncType }`}</td>
        <td>Lyrics were applied, including an empty array</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>braccato:line-click</code></td>
        <td>{`{ timeS }`}</td>
        <td>A line was clicked. With a bound source, the seek has already happened</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>braccato:scroll-state</code></td>
        <td>{`{ userScrolling }`}</td>
        <td>Autoscroll stopped following the song, or started again</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>braccato:error</code></td>
        <td>{`{ phase, error }`}</td>
        <td>Something failed while connecting, resolving the source, or applying lyrics or a theme</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Errors are dispatched one microtask after they happen, so a listener added in the same task that
    inserts the element still receives them. If you attach listeners later, read <code>status</code>
    instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Theming</h2>
  <p>
    A theme is a CSS string written to <code>theme</code>. Settings that change behavior go in
    comments with a <code>blyrics-</code> prefix. Everything else is ordinary CSS.
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">{`view.theme = \`
  /* blyrics-target-scroll-pos-ratio = 0.5; */
  /* blyrics-long-word-threshold = 900; */

  .blyrics-container {
    --blyrics-font-size: 3rem;
    --blyrics-lyric-active-color: white;
    --blyrics-lyric-inactive-color: rgb(255 255 255 / 0.3);
  }
\`;`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>variables.css</code> declares every <code>--blyrics-*</code> custom property. The class names
    (<code>blyrics-container</code>, <code>blyrics--line</code>, <code>blyrics--active</code>,
    <code>blyrics--word</code> and others) are public API. Import them from
    <code>@braccato/core/constants</code> instead of typing them. Each word also carries
    <code>data-word-state</code> (<code>upcoming</code>, <code>active</code> or <code>past</code>).
  </p>
  <p>
    If you write themes in RICS, compile them to CSS with <code>@braccato/rics</code> first.
  </p>

  <h2>React example</h2>
  <p>
    The element is a normal custom element, so in React you set its properties through a ref. This
    example drives the clock from the player and seeks on line click.
  </p>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">{`import "@braccato/core/element";
import "@braccato/core/styles/variables.css";
import "@braccato/core/styles/lyrics.css";
import "@braccato/core/styles/instrumental.css";
import { useEffect, useRef } from "react";

export function Lyrics({ lyrics, getCurrentTime, getPlaying, onSeek }) {
  const ref = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    ref.current.lyrics = lyrics;
  }, [lyrics]);

  useEffect(() => {
    let frame;
    const tick = () => {
      ref.current.currentTime = getCurrentTime();
      ref.current.playing = getPlaying();
      frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    };
    frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    return () => cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
  }, [getCurrentTime, getPlaying]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const el = ref.current;
    const seek = (event) => onSeek(event.detail.timeS);
    const noteUserScroll = () => el.renderer?.noteUserScroll();
    el.addEventListener("braccato:line-click", seek);
    el.addEventListener("scroll", noteUserScroll, { passive: true });
    return () => {
      el.removeEventListener("braccato:line-click", seek);
      el.removeEventListener("scroll", noteUserScroll);
    };
  }, [onSeek]);

  return <braccato-lyrics ref={ref} style={{ display: "block", height: 600, overflowY: "auto" }} />;
}`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      One renderer per document. The theme and scroll padding are written per document, so two
      elements given different themes both report <code>theme-conflict</code>. Both keep rendering.
    </li>
    <li>
      Load one copy of the package per page. The first copy to load registers the element names.
    </li>
    <li>
      A browser extension's isolated world has no custom element registry. Use the element from the
      page's world, or call <code>createLyricsRenderer</code> from <code>@braccato/core</code> directly.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
